# Test: ddos-detect -> trafficusage -> ddos-local end-to-end (Phase 1 "Unblock").
#
# VALIDATES: cp-survival-5-detect-5 AC-1 -- on a volumetric flood the detector
#            queries trafficusage, fills the victim DstPrefix, and emits a
#            populated AttackDetected.
# VALIDATES: cp-survival-5-detect-5 AC-2 -- ddos-local (enforce) installs an
#            nftables drop rule for the attacked destination.
# PREVENTS:  the mitigation chain staying inert (detector emits an empty target,
#            responders refuse to act).
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test (option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf); NOT executable on
#   the darwin dev host. Reworked per spec-fixit-ddos-test-infra AC-2a: the
#   daemon.pid/daemon.ready handshake is KEPT (it is armed for a background `ze`
#   -- runner_exec_util.go:125-130, runner_exec.go:702-705,777-791 -- and polled
#   green today by test/vrrp/vrrp-instance-up.ci:123). The native fixture uses
#   bounded Poll waits for daemon readiness and kernel state. The nft readback
#   remains in the root driver, which the runner keeps privileged
#   (runner_exec.go:740-744).
#   The static surface (config leaves, nft table name `ddos-local`, log strings)
#   is grounded in source; these runtime behaviors are proven by the QEMU run:
#     - the iface rate collector reports `lo` RxPackets to the detector
#       (rate.go ListInterfaces feeds all kernel links to onRate),
#     - trafficusage TCX attaches to `lo` and records the destination in
#       egress-ips (track-ip),
#     - the loopback flood sustains > absolute-floor (1000) pps across >=2
#       collect ticks so the state machine activates.
#   If the trigger proves hard to drive on loopback, switch the flooded device
#   to a dedicated veth created before the daemon starts.

# This test needs a fully privileged host, not merely Linux, and needs TWO
# distinct capability families -- which is why it declares both.
#   - bpf (CAP_BPF + CAP_SYS_RESOURCE): traffic-usage calls
#     rlimit.RemoveMemlock() and then link.AttachTCX()
#     (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go:33,46). On the unprivileged
#     GitHub runner the rlimit call failed with "need CAP_BPF/CAP_SYS_RESOURCE",
#     eBPF accounting ran inert, and no incident was ever opened.
#   - net-admin: AttachTCX programs the interface's tc hooks, a privileged
#     network operation.
# Declaring only one of the two would fail OPEN: a host holding just that one
# passes the gate and the test fails exactly as it did with no marker at all
# (ai/rules/fail-closed-guards.md, ai/rules/qemu-testing.md).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
# Serialize against every other ddos test: they all flood the SAME loopback
# interface and the detector picks the victim by top-destination-bytes over that
# interface, so a sibling's concurrent flood is indistinguishable from this
# test's own. Rationale in full: test/plugin/ddos-direction.ci.
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.detect:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.local:value=info
option=timeout:value=40s


stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
    detect {
        enabled true;
        absolute-floor 1000;
        confirm-duration 1;
        startup-grace 0;
        baseline-window 10;
    }
    local {
        response-level enforce;
    }
}

traffic {
    usage {
        enabled true;
        track-ip true;
        interfaces {
            interface lo {
                enabled true;
            }
        }
    }
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-detect-mitigate:timeout=35s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=ddos-local
expect=stdout:contains=127.0.0.4
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: drop rule installed
